\begin{abstract}
Let $p$ be a strictly positive probability distribution on a finite set $\Omega$ (a base or reference policy), let $r:\Omega\to\R$ be a bounded reward with $|r|\le R$, and let
\[
\pi_\beta(y)=\frac{p(y)e^{r(y)/\beta}}{Z_\beta},\qquad Z_\beta=\sum_{y\in\Omega}p(y)e^{r(y)/\beta},
\]
be the exact maximizer of the KL-regularized objective $\E_\pi[r]-\beta\KL(\pi\Vert p)$. For an arbitrary \emph{audit statistic} $f:\Omega\to\R$ define the \emph{audit gap} $G(\beta)=\E_{\pi_\beta}[f]-\E_p[f]$.

We prove that the audit gap is, exactly at every regularization strength, the covariance of $f$ with the likelihood ratio $\pi_\beta/p$; that this covariance linearizes with an explicit constant,
\[
\Bigl|G(\beta)-\frac{\Cov_p(r,f)}{\beta}\Bigr|\le 24\Bigl(\frac{R}{\beta}\Bigr)^{2}\sigma_p(f)\qquad(0<R\le\beta);
\]
and that the leading coefficient is attained, $\beta G(\beta)\to\Cov_p(r,f)$. Consequently a statistic uncorrelated with the reward drifts only at order $\beta^{-2}$.

The two-sided envelope this produces yields a \emph{sharp hacking threshold}. Defining the critical regularization strength at auditor tolerance $\varepsilon$ as $\beta_c(\varepsilon)=\sup\{\beta\ge R:|G(\beta)|\ge\varepsilon\}$, we prove an explicit two-sided window $(1-\delta)|\Cov_p(r,f)|/\varepsilon\le\beta_c(\varepsilon)\le(1+\delta)|\Cov_p(r,f)|/\varepsilon$ for all sufficiently small $\varepsilon$, hence
\[
\varepsilon\,\beta_c(\varepsilon)\longrightarrow\bigl|\Cov_p(r,f)\bigr|\qquad(\varepsilon\downarrow0).
\]
Reward hacking of a statistic at tolerance $\varepsilon$ switches on exactly at $\beta_c(\varepsilon)=(1+o(1))|\Cov_p(r,f)|/\varepsilon$.

We extend the expansion to second order, where the coefficient is the \emph{skew covariance} $\SkewCov_p(r,f)=\E_p[(r-\E_p r)^2(f-\E_p f)]$, with remainder $40(R/\beta)^3\sigma_p(f)$ and exact limit $\beta^2(G(\beta)-\Cov_p(r,f)/\beta)\to\SkewCov_p(r,f)/2$. Finally we show both orders are attained: on the symmetric two-point model the gap is exactly $\tanh(R/\beta)$, and on the biased two-point model $\SkewCov=8R^2q(1-q)(1-2q)\neq0$, so the exponent $2$ in the first-order law cannot be improved to any $o(\beta^{-2})$.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords.} reward hacking; KL-regularized policy optimization; Gibbs measure; linear response; covariance; phase transition; alignment auditing.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The problem}
Fine-tuning a generative model against a learned reward reliably produces two effects: the reward goes up, and other, unmonitored quantities move. The second effect is variously called \emph{reward hacking}, \emph{specification gaming}, or \emph{proxy drift}, and it is usually discussed qualitatively---as though the drift depended on the optimizer's search dynamics, or on the discovery of ``loopholes'' in the reward.

This paper argues, and proves, that under the standard KL-regularized objective the drift is not dynamical at all. It is a fixed, computable functional of the base model: the covariance of the drifting statistic with the reward. Nothing about the optimizer, the training trajectory, or the number of gradient steps enters the leading term.

\subsection{Setting and conventions}
Throughout, $\Omega$ is a finite nonempty set of responses. A \emph{distribution} is $p:\Omega\to\R$ with $p\ge0$ and $\sum_y p(y)=1$; it is a \emph{positive distribution} if $p(y)>0$ for all $y$. We write
\[
\E_p[f]=\sum_y p(y)f(y),\qquad \Var_p(f)=\E_p[(f-\E_p f)^2],\qquad \sigma_p(f)=\sqrt{\Var_p(f)},
\]
\[
\Cov_p(f,g)=\E_p\bigl[(f-\E_p f)(g-\E_p g)\bigr].
\]
The reward $r:\Omega\to\R$ satisfies $|r(y)|\le R$ for all $y$; the parameter $\beta>0$ is the KL penalty coefficient, so that \emph{large} $\beta$ corresponds to a \emph{weak, conservative} update. All results are stated in the perturbative regime $R\le\beta$; the constants are explicit and not optimized.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{enumerate}
\item An exact identity (Theorem~\ref{thm:exact}): $G(\beta)=\Cov_p(\pi_\beta/p,f)$ at every $\beta$, with no error term.
\item The first-order law (Theorem~\ref{thm:first}): $|G(\beta)-\Cov_p(r,f)/\beta|\le24(R/\beta)^2\sigma_p(f)$, with the alignment corollary that uncorrelated statistics move only at order $\beta^{-2}$ (Corollary~\ref{cor:safe1}).
\item Exactness of the coefficient (Theorem~\ref{thm:rate1}): $\beta G(\beta)\to\Cov_p(r,f)$.
\item A sharp hacking threshold (Theorems~\ref{thm:window}--\ref{thm:sharp}).
\item The second-order coefficient (Theorems~\ref{thm:second}--\ref{thm:rate2}): the skew covariance, with cubic remainder and second-order safety.
\item Optimality (Section~\ref{sec:attained}): closed forms on two-point models showing both the $\beta^{-1}$ constant and the $\beta^{-2}$ exponent are attained.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Relation to classical linear response}
Readers from statistical physics will recognize the shape of Theorem~\ref{thm:first}. A Gibbs measure tilted by a small field responds, to first order in the field strength, through the covariance of the observable with the field---the fluctuation--dissipation relation. The contribution here is not the heuristic but the quantitative apparatus: two-sided bounds with explicit constants in the exact regime that governs practical fine-tuning, and, on top of them, a genuine sharp-threshold theorem for the critical regularization strength, which is not a classical linear-response statement.

\section{Moments of the reference policy}

Let $p$ be a distribution on the finite set $\Omega$.

\begin{lemma}[Raw-moment form]\label{lem:raw}
$\Cov_p(f,g)=\E_p[fg]-\E_p[f]\E_p[g]$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand $(f-\E_p f)(g-\E_p g)$ pointwise and use $\sum_y p(y)=1$ three times.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Bilinearity]\label{lem:bilin}
$\Cov_p$ is symmetric, additive in each argument, and $\Cov_p(g/c,f)=\Cov_p(g,f)/c$ for $c\neq0$. Moreover $\Cov_p(f,f)=\Var_p(f)\ge0$, and $\Cov_p(g,f)$ is unchanged if a constant is added to $g$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Pair representation of the variance]\label{lem:pair}
$\displaystyle \Var_p(f)=\frac12\sum_{x\in\Omega}\sum_{y\in\Omega}p(x)p(y)\bigl(f(x)-f(y)\bigr)^2 .$
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand $(f(x)-f(y))^2=f(x)^2-2f(x)f(y)+f(y)^2$, sum in $y$ then in $x$ using $\sum p=1$, and compare with $\Var_p(f)=\E_p[f^2]-(\E_p f)^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Oscillation controls the standard deviation]\label{lem:osc}
If $|g(x)-g(y)|\le c$ for all $x,y\in\Omega$, with $c\ge0$, then $\sigma_p(g)\le c$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:pair} each summand obeys $p(x)p(y)(g(x)-g(y))^2\le p(x)p(y)c^2$, and $\sum_{x,y}p(x)p(y)c^2=c^2$; hence $\Var_p(g)\le c^2/2\le c^2$. Take square roots.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Cauchy--Schwarz]\label{lem:cs}
$|\Cov_p(f,g)|\le\sigma_p(f)\sigma_p(g)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply the discrete Cauchy--Schwarz inequality to $y\mapsto\sqrt{p(y)}(f(y)-\E_p f)$ and $y\mapsto\sqrt{p(y)}(g(y)-\E_p g)$, whose inner product is $\Cov_p(f,g)$ and whose squared norms are $\Var_p(f)$ and $\Var_p(g)$.
\end{proof}

Lemmas~\ref{lem:osc} and~\ref{lem:cs} combine into the workhorse of the paper: \emph{if a reweighting function $g$ oscillates by at most $c$, then it can move the mean of any statistic $f$ by at most $c\,\sigma_p(f)$.}

\section{The aligned policy and the exact covariance identity}

\begin{definition}[Partition function, Gibbs policy, likelihood ratio]
For $\beta\neq0$ set
\[
Z_\beta=\sum_y p(y)e^{r(y)/\beta},\qquad \pi_\beta(y)=\frac{p(y)e^{r(y)/\beta}}{Z_\beta},\qquad L_\beta(y)=\frac{e^{r(y)/\beta}}{Z_\beta}.
\]
Then $\pi_\beta(y)=p(y)L_\beta(y)$, and $\pi_\beta$ is a probability distribution.
\end{definition}

$\pi_\beta$ is the unique maximizer of $\E_\pi[r]-\beta\KL(\pi\Vert p)$ over distributions absolutely continuous with respect to $p$; this is the standard closed form for KL-regularized policy optimization and is the reason the family is the right object of study.

\begin{lemma}[Normalization]\label{lem:norm}
If $p$ is a positive distribution then $Z_\beta>0$ and $\E_p[L_\beta]=1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[The audit gap is exactly a covariance]\label{thm:exact}
For every positive distribution $p$, every $\beta\neq0$, and every statistic $f$,
\[
G(\beta)=\E_{\pi_\beta}[f]-\E_p[f]=\Cov_p\bigl(L_\beta,f\bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:raw}, $\Cov_p(L_\beta,f)=\E_p[L_\beta f]-\E_p[L_\beta]\E_p[f]$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:norm} the second term is $\E_p[f]$, and $\E_p[L_\beta f]=\sum_y p(y)L_\beta(y)f(y)=\sum_y\pi_\beta(y)f(y)=\E_{\pi_\beta}[f]$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:exact} is exact---no perturbative hypothesis, no error term. It already contains the qualitative message: \emph{what fine-tuning moves is what covaries, under the base model, with the reweighting fine-tuning applies.}

\section{The first-order law}

\begin{lemma}[The partition function is close to one]\label{lem:Z}
If $p$ is a positive distribution, $|r|\le R$ and $0<R\le\beta$, then $\tfrac13\le Z_\beta\le3$ and $|Z_\beta-1|\le 3R/\beta$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For the lower bound, $r(y)/\beta\ge-1$ pointwise, so $Z_\beta\ge e^{-1}\ge1/3$ (using $e\le3$). For the two-sided bound, write $Z_\beta-1=\sum_y p(y)(e^{r(y)/\beta}-1)$ and apply $|e^u-1|\le 2|u|$ for $|u|\le1$, with $|r(y)/\beta|\le R/\beta\le1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[The likelihood ratio is its own linearization]\label{lem:lin}
Under the hypotheses of Lemma~\ref{lem:Z}, for all $x,y\in\Omega$,
\[
\Bigl|\Bigl(L_\beta(x)-\frac{r(x)}{\beta}\Bigr)-\Bigl(L_\beta(y)-\frac{r(y)}{\beta}\Bigr)\Bigr|\le 24\Bigl(\frac{R}{\beta}\Bigr)^{2}.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Write $u_z=r(z)/\beta$, so $|u_z|\le R/\beta\le1$. Algebraically,
\[
\bigl(L_\beta(x)-u_x\bigr)-\bigl(L_\beta(y)-u_y\bigr)
=\frac{\bigl[(e^{u_x}-1-u_x)-(e^{u_y}-1-u_y)\bigr]+(1-Z_\beta)(u_x-u_y)}{Z_\beta}.
\]
The Taylor bound $|e^u-1-u|\le u^2$ for $|u|\le1$ gives $|(e^{u_x}-1-u_x)-(e^{u_y}-1-u_y)|\le 2(R/\beta)^2$; and by Lemma~\ref{lem:Z} with $|u_x-u_y|\le 2R/\beta$ we get $|(1-Z_\beta)(u_x-u_y)|\le 6(R/\beta)^2$. The numerator is at most $8(R/\beta)^2$; dividing by $Z_\beta\ge1/3$ gives $24(R/\beta)^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[First-order reward-hacking law]\label{thm:first}
Let $p$ be a positive distribution on a finite set, $|r|\le R$, and $0<R\le\beta$. Then for every statistic $f$,
\[
\Bigl|\;\E_{\pi_\beta}[f]-\E_p[f]-\frac{\Cov_p(r,f)}{\beta}\;\Bigr|\le 24\Bigl(\frac{R}{\beta}\Bigr)^{2}\sigma_p(f).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:exact}, $G(\beta)=\Cov_p(L_\beta,f)$. Split $L_\beta=(r/\beta)+(L_\beta-r/\beta)$ and use Lemma~\ref{lem:bilin}:
\[
G(\beta)=\frac{\Cov_p(r,f)}{\beta}+\Cov_p\Bigl(L_\beta-\frac{r}{\beta},\,f\Bigr).
\]
The residual is at most $\sigma_p(L_\beta-r/\beta)\sigma_p(f)$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:cs}, and $\sigma_p(L_\beta-r/\beta)\le24(R/\beta)^2$ by Lemmas~\ref{lem:osc} and~\ref{lem:lin}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Uncorrelated statistics are second-order safe]\label{cor:safe1}
If additionally $\Cov_p(r,f)=0$, then $|\E_{\pi_\beta}[f]-\E_p[f]|\le 24(R/\beta)^2\sigma_p(f)$.
\end{corollary}

Reward hacking, at leading order, is not a generic perturbation of all measurable behaviour: it is a perturbation \emph{along the reward direction} in $L^2(p)$. Statistics orthogonal to the reward are quadratically protected.

\begin{remark}[Scale invariance]
Both sides of Theorem~\ref{thm:first} are homogeneous of degree $1$ in $f$ and invariant under $(r,\beta)\mapsto(\lambda r,\lambda\beta)$. Only the dimensionless \emph{effective tilt} $R/\beta$ controls the accuracy of the expansion.
\end{remark}

\section{The covariance is exactly the hacking rate}

Write $K=24R^2\sigma_p(f)\ge0$, so that Theorem~\ref{thm:first} reads $|G(\beta)-\Cov_p(r,f)/\beta|\le K/\beta^2$.

\begin{theorem}[Exact first-order rate]\label{thm:rate1}
If $p$ is a positive distribution and $|r|\le R$, then $\displaystyle\lim_{\beta\to\infty}\beta G(\beta)=\Cov_p(r,f)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For $\beta\ge\max(R,1)$, $|\beta G(\beta)-\Cov_p(r,f)|=\beta|G(\beta)-\Cov_p(r,f)/\beta|\le K/\beta\to0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Two-sided envelope]\label{cor:env}
For $0<R\le\beta$,
\[
\frac{|\Cov_p(r,f)|}{\beta}-\frac{K}{\beta^2}\;\le\;|G(\beta)|\;\le\;\frac{|\Cov_p(r,f)|}{\beta}+\frac{K}{\beta^2}.
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Both are the reverse triangle inequality $\bigl||a|-|b|\bigr|\le|a-b|$ with $a=G(\beta)$, $b=\Cov_p(r,f)/\beta$.
\end{proof}

The \emph{lower} envelope converts a safety statement into an inevitability statement: any statistic with nonzero reward covariance is guaranteed to move, by a computable amount, as soon as $\beta\gg K/|\Cov_p(r,f)|$.

\section{The sharp hacking threshold}

\begin{definition}[Hacked set and critical strength]
For $\varepsilon>0$,
\[
H(\varepsilon)=\{\beta\in\R: R\le\beta\ \text{and}\ |G(\beta)|\ge\varepsilon\},\qquad \beta_c(\varepsilon)=\sup H(\varepsilon).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Hacked below the threshold]\label{prop:below}
Let $\delta>0$, $C:=|\Cov_p(r,f)|>0$, $0<R\le\beta$, and suppose
$2K\le\delta C\beta$ and $\varepsilon\beta\le(1-\delta)C$. Then $|G(\beta)|>\varepsilon$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Corollary~\ref{cor:env} it suffices that $\varepsilon\beta^2<C\beta-K$. The first hypothesis gives $K\le\tfrac{\delta}{2}C\beta$ and the second $\varepsilon\beta^2\le(1-\delta)C\beta$; hence $C\beta-K-\varepsilon\beta^2\ge\tfrac{\delta}{2}C\beta>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Safe above the threshold]\label{prop:above}
Under $0<R\le\beta$, $2K\le\delta C\beta$ and $(1+\delta)C\le\varepsilon\beta$, one has $|G(\beta)|\le\varepsilon$; with the last inequality strict, $|G(\beta)|<\varepsilon$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Corollary~\ref{cor:env} it suffices that $C\beta+K\le\varepsilon\beta^2$, which follows by the same two substitutions.
\end{proof}

The multiplicative formulation ($\varepsilon\beta\le(1-\delta)C$) rather than the divided one keeps the estimates uniform and free of case analysis on denominators.

\begin{theorem}[The hacked set is bounded]\label{thm:bdd}
Assume $p$ positive, $|r|\le R$, $R>0$, $\varepsilon>0$, $\delta\in(0,1)$, $C>0$, and
\begin{equation}\tag{$\star$}\label{eq:star}
2K\varepsilon\le\delta(1-\delta)C^2 .
\end{equation}
Then $H(\varepsilon)\subseteq(-\infty,(1+\delta)C/\varepsilon]$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Suppose $\beta\in H(\varepsilon)$ with $\beta>(1+\delta)C/\varepsilon$, so $(1+\delta)C<\varepsilon\beta$ and in particular $(1-\delta)C<\varepsilon\beta$. Multiplying the latter by $\delta C>0$ and combining with \eqref{eq:star} gives $2K\varepsilon\le\delta(1-\delta)C^2\le(\delta C\beta)\varepsilon$, so $2K\le\delta C\beta$. Proposition~\ref{prop:above} (strict form) yields $|G(\beta)|<\varepsilon$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The window is nonempty]\label{thm:nonempty}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:bdd} together with
\begin{equation}\tag{$\star\star$}\label{eq:starstar}
\varepsilon R\le(1-\delta)C,
\end{equation}
the point $\beta^-:=(1-\delta)C/\varepsilon$ belongs to $H(\varepsilon)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
\eqref{eq:starstar} gives $R\le\beta^-$; $\varepsilon\beta^-=(1-\delta)C$ exactly; and \eqref{eq:star} gives $2K\le\delta C\beta^-$. Proposition~\ref{prop:below} applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Critical-strength window]\label{thm:window}
Under \eqref{eq:star} and \eqref{eq:starstar},
\[
(1-\delta)\frac{|\Cov_p(r,f)|}{\varepsilon}\;\le\;\beta_c(\varepsilon)\;\le\;(1+\delta)\frac{|\Cov_p(r,f)|}{\varepsilon},
\]
equivalently $\bigl|\varepsilon\beta_c(\varepsilon)-|\Cov_p(r,f)|\bigr|\le\delta|\Cov_p(r,f)|$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:bdd} bounds $H(\varepsilon)$ above, so the supremum exists and obeys the upper bound; Theorem~\ref{thm:nonempty} exhibits the element $\beta^-$, giving the lower bound.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp threshold]\label{thm:sharp}
If $p$ is a positive distribution, $|r|\le R$ with $R>0$, and $\Cov_p(r,f)\neq0$, then
\[
\lim_{\varepsilon\downarrow0}\varepsilon\,\beta_c(\varepsilon)=\bigl|\Cov_p(r,f)\bigr|,
\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad
\beta_c(\varepsilon)=(1+o(1))\frac{|\Cov_p(r,f)|}{\varepsilon}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Given $\eta>0$, put $C=|\Cov_p(r,f)|$ and $\delta=\min\{1/2,\eta/(2C)\}$, so $\delta\in(0,1)$ and $\delta C\le\eta/2$. Both \eqref{eq:star} and \eqref{eq:starstar} hold for every
\[
0<\varepsilon<\min\Bigl\{\frac{(1-\delta)C}{R},\ \frac{\delta(1-\delta)C^2}{2K+1}\Bigr\},
\]
and Theorem~\ref{thm:window} then gives $|\varepsilon\beta_c(\varepsilon)-C|\le\delta C\le\eta/2<\eta$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Why a supremum, and not a crossing point]\label{rem:sup}
It is tempting to define the critical strength as the unique $\beta$ at which $|G(\beta)|$ crosses $\varepsilon$. This is not available: $|G|$ need not be monotone in $\beta$ for general $r$ and $f$. Corollary~\ref{cor:env} constrains $|G|$ only to the band $|\Cov|/\beta\pm K/\beta^2$, inside which the gap may oscillate. The order-theoretic definition $\beta_c=\sup H(\varepsilon)$ sidesteps this: it needs no regularity of $G$, only the two one-sided propositions, and delivers the same asymptotics. This is a transferable lesson: when the natural crossing point may fail to be unique, define the threshold as the supremum of the hacked region and prove a two-sided sandwich.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[Operational reading]
To keep a monitored statistic $f$ within tolerance $\varepsilon$ one must take $\beta\gtrsim|\Cov_p(r,f)|/\varepsilon$, and this is tight. Halving the tolerance doubles the required regularization. Since $\Cov_p(r,f)$ is a base-model quantity, the calibration can be done before any fine-tuning takes place.
\end{remark}

\section{Second order: the skew covariance}

\begin{definition}[Skew covariance]
$\SkewCov_p(r,f)=\E_p\bigl[(r-\E_p r)^2(f-\E_p f)\bigr]$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:skew}
$\SkewCov_p(r,f)=\Cov_p(r^2,f)-2\E_p[r]\Cov_p(r,f)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand $(r-\E_p r)^2=r^2-2\E_p[r]r+(\E_p r)^2$, pair each term against the centred statistic $f-\E_p f$, and use that pairing a constant against a centred function gives $0$.
\end{proof}

Define the second-order model of the likelihood ratio
\[
Q_\beta(y)=\frac{r(y)}{\beta}+\frac12\Bigl(\frac{r(y)}{\beta}\Bigr)^{2}-\frac{\E_p[r]}{\beta}\cdot\frac{r(y)}{\beta},
\]
normalized to have no constant term (constants are invisible inside a covariance). The three terms are the second-order Taylor expansion of $e^{r/\beta}$ divided by the expansion $Z_\beta\approx1+\E_p[r]/\beta$.

\begin{lemma}[The quadratic model reproduces both orders]\label{lem:quad}
For $\beta\neq0$,
$\displaystyle \Cov_p(Q_\beta,f)=\frac{\Cov_p(r,f)}{\beta}+\frac{\SkewCov_p(r,f)}{2\beta^2}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write each covariance as $\sum_y p(y)g(y)(f(y)-\E_p f)$ (only one argument need be centred); linearity in $g$ gives
$\Cov_p(Q_\beta,f)=\beta^{-1}\Cov_p(r,f)+\tfrac{1}{2}\beta^{-2}\Cov_p(r^2,f)-\E_p[r]\beta^{-2}\Cov_p(r,f)$, which equals the claim by Lemma~\ref{lem:skew}.
\end{proof}

The cubic oscillation estimate parallels Lemma~\ref{lem:lin}, using $|e^u-1-u-u^2/2|\le|u|^3$ for $|u|\le1$ together with $|Z_\beta-1-\E_p[r]/\beta|\le(R/\beta)^2$; the outcome is that $L_\beta-Q_\beta$ has oscillation at most $40(R/\beta)^3$.

\begin{theorem}[Second-order law]\label{thm:second}
For $p$ positive, $|r|\le R$ and $0<R\le\beta$,
\[
\Bigl|\;\E_{\pi_\beta}[f]-\E_p[f]-\frac{\Cov_p(r,f)}{\beta}-\frac{\SkewCov_p(r,f)}{2\beta^2}\;\Bigr|\le 40\Bigl(\frac{R}{\beta}\Bigr)^{3}\sigma_p(f).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$G(\beta)=\Cov_p(L_\beta,f)=\Cov_p(Q_\beta,f)+\Cov_p(L_\beta-Q_\beta,f)$; substitute Lemma~\ref{lem:quad} and bound the second term by $\sigma_p(L_\beta-Q_\beta)\sigma_p(f)\le40(R/\beta)^3\sigma_p(f)$ using Lemmas~\ref{lem:osc},~\ref{lem:cs} and the cubic oscillation estimate.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact second-order rate]\label{thm:rate2}
$\displaystyle\lim_{\beta\to\infty}\beta^2\Bigl(G(\beta)-\frac{\Cov_p(r,f)}{\beta}\Bigr)=\frac{\SkewCov_p(r,f)}{2}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Second-order safety]\label{cor:safe2}
If $\Cov_p(r,f)=0$ and $\SkewCov_p(r,f)=0$, then $|G(\beta)|\le40(R/\beta)^3\sigma_p(f)$.
\end{corollary}

Thus there is a hierarchy of \emph{audit invariants}: a statistic is first-order safe iff it is $L^2(p)$-orthogonal to $r$, and second-order safe iff it is in addition orthogonal to $(r-\E_p r)^2$.

\section{Both orders are attained}\label{sec:attained}

\subsection{The symmetric two-point model}
Take $\Omega=\{+,-\}$, $p(+)=p(-)=1/2$, $r(\pm)=\pm R$, $f(\pm)=\pm1$.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:tanh}
$\E_p[r]=\E_p[f]=0$, $\sigma_p(f)=1$, $\Cov_p(r,f)=R$, $Z_\beta=\cosh(R/\beta)$, and $G(\beta)=\tanh(R/\beta)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{corollary}
$\beta G(\beta)=\beta\tanh(R/\beta)\to R=\Cov_p(r,f)$, and $G(\beta)>0$ for all $\beta,R>0$: the aligned policy always moves the aligned statistic in the reward's direction.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Closed-form threshold]
Since $\beta\mapsto\tanh(R/\beta)$ is strictly decreasing and positive, for $0<\varepsilon<\tanh 1$ the hacked set is exactly $[R,\,R/\artanh\varepsilon]$ and
\[
\beta_c(\varepsilon)=\frac{R}{\artanh\varepsilon}=\frac{R}{\varepsilon}\Bigl(1-\frac{\varepsilon^2}{3}+O(\varepsilon^4)\Bigr),
\]
so $\varepsilon\beta_c(\varepsilon)\to R=|\Cov_p(r,f)|$, in agreement with Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}.
\end{corollary}

By symmetry $\SkewCov=0$ here (the centred reward squared is constant), consistent with $\tanh$ being odd: the expansion is $R/\beta-R^3/(3\beta^3)+\cdots$, with no $\beta^{-2}$ term.

\subsection{The biased two-point model: optimality of the exponent}
Now let $p(+)=q$, $p(-)=1-q$ with $q\in(0,1)$, keeping $r(\pm)=\pm R$, $f(\pm)=\pm1$.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:biased}
$\E_p[f]=2q-1$, $\E_p[r]=R(2q-1)$, and
\[
\Cov_p(r,f)=4Rq(1-q),\qquad \SkewCov_p(r,f)=8R^2q(1-q)(1-2q).
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\E_p[rf]=qR+(1-q)R=R$, so $\Cov=R-R(2q-1)^2=4Rq(1-q)$. For the skew covariance use Lemma~\ref{lem:skew} with $r^2\equiv R^2$ constant, so $\Cov_p(r^2,f)=0$ and $\SkewCov=-2R(2q-1)\cdot4Rq(1-q)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The $\beta^{-2}$ remainder is genuinely present]\label{cor:optimal}
For $q\in(0,1)$,
\[
\beta^2\Bigl(G(\beta)-\frac{\Cov_p(r,f)}{\beta}\Bigr)\longrightarrow 4R^2q(1-q)(1-2q),
\]
which is nonzero whenever $R>0$ and $q\neq1/2$. Hence the remainder in Theorem~\ref{thm:first} is \textbf{not} $o(\beta^{-2})$, and the exponent $2$ there is optimal.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:rate2} with Proposition~\ref{prop:biased}; if the rescaled remainder tended to $0$, uniqueness of limits would force $\SkewCov_p(r,f)=0$.
\end{proof}

Interpretively: it is \emph{skewness of the base model} that opens the second-order hacking channel. A symmetric base model is protected at order $\beta^{-2}$; a biased one is not.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{A. Audit-gap oracle.}
Given $p,r,f,\beta$: compute $w_y=p_ye^{r_y/\beta}$, normalize, return $\sum_y\pi_\beta(y)f(y)-\sum_y p(y)f(y)$. Cost $O(|\Omega|)$. In practice $\Omega$ is enormous and the sums are Monte-Carlo estimates over base-model samples with self-normalized importance weights $e^{r/\beta}$.

\paragraph{B. Covariance audit ranking.}
Given candidate statistics $f_1,\dots,f_m$, compute $c_i=\Cov_p(r,f_i)$ and $s_i=\SkewCov_p(r,f_i)$ from a single scored sample of the base model; rank by $|c_i|$, breaking ties on $|s_i|$. By Theorems~\ref{thm:first} and~\ref{thm:second} this ranks the statistics by predicted drift with errors $O(\beta^{-2})$ and $O(\beta^{-3})$. Cost $O(mn)$ for $n$ samples.

\paragraph{C. Critical-strength solver.}
Compute $\beta_c(\varepsilon)$ by bisection on the envelope-certified bracket $[(1-\delta)C/\varepsilon,(1+\delta)C/\varepsilon]$ from Theorem~\ref{thm:window}, testing membership in $H(\varepsilon)$ with algorithm A. No unbounded search is needed; $C/\varepsilon$ is itself a first guess with relative error $O(\varepsilon)$ in general and $O(\varepsilon^2)$ on the symmetric two-point model.

\section{Applications}

\paragraph{Pre-training-time audit prediction.}
The pair $(\Cov_p(r,f),\SkewCov_p(r,f))$ is a complete two-term predictor of drift. Both are base-model expectations, so all monitored metrics can be ranked by predicted movement before a single fine-tuning step.

\paragraph{Principled KL-penalty selection.}
Given monitored statistics $\mathcal F$ and tolerance $\varepsilon$, Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp} turns the choice of $\beta$ into an inequality: $\beta\ge(1+\delta)\max_{f\in\mathcal F}|\Cov_p(r,f)|/\varepsilon$ suffices, and by Theorem~\ref{thm:window} nothing substantially smaller does.

\paragraph{Hardened metrics.}
The first-order-safe statistics form the hyperplane $r^\perp\subset L^2(p)$. Any statistic can be hardened by projection,
\[
\tilde f=f-\frac{\Cov_p(r,f)}{\Var_p(r)}\,r,
\]
after which Corollary~\ref{cor:safe1} applies; orthogonalizing further against $(r-\E_p r)^2$ buys safety through order $\beta^{-2}$ by Corollary~\ref{cor:safe2}.

\paragraph{A dual, adversarial reading.}
The same decomposition tells an adversary where to hide: behaviour supported in the orthogonal complement of $\mathrm{span}\{r,(r-\E_p r)^2\}$ moves hardened metrics only at order $\beta^{-3}$. Reward-orthogonal directions should be treated as \emph{unmonitored by construction}, not as \emph{safe}.

\paragraph{Beyond alignment.}
Nothing in the proofs uses the interpretation of $r$ as a reward. The same statements govern importance-weighted estimators, exponential tilting in rare-event simulation, and any setting where a base measure is reweighted by a bounded exponential factor.

\section{Discussion and limitations}

\paragraph{Constants.} The constants $24$ and $40$ arise from crude but robust estimates and are not optimal; the symmetric two-point model shows the true second-order remainder can vanish. What is optimal is the shape: $\beta^{-2}\sigma_p(f)$ at first order (Corollary~\ref{cor:optimal}) and $\beta^{-3}\sigma_p(f)$ at second.

\paragraph{Regime.} All bounds assume $R\le\beta$. Outside this regime the exponential is genuinely nonlinear: as $\beta\downarrow0$ the Gibbs policy concentrates on the argmax of $r$ and the audit gap saturates at $\max f-\E_p f$ rather than growing like $1/\beta$. The threshold theorem is asymptotic in $\varepsilon\downarrow0$ precisely because small $\varepsilon$ pushes $\beta_c$ deep into the perturbative regime.

\paragraph{Exactness of the Gibbs form.} We analyze the exact optimum of the KL-regularized objective; real pipelines approximate it. The analysis is uniform over the whole family $\{\pi_\beta\}$, so any policy that equals $\pi_\beta$ for some effective $\beta$ inherits the bounds.

\paragraph{Finiteness.} $\Omega$ is finite. Extension to general measurable $\Omega$ with $|r|\le R$ and $f\in L^2(p)$ is expected to be routine.

\paragraph{Non-monotonicity.} As stressed in Remark~\ref{rem:sup}, $\beta_c(\varepsilon)$ is a supremum, and $H(\varepsilon)$ need not be an interval outside the perturbative window.

\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{Cumulant hierarchy of audit invariants.} $\Cov_p(r,f)$ and $\SkewCov_p(r,f)$ appear to be the first two members of one family: the $k$-th order hacking coefficient should be a mixed cumulant of $r^{\otimes k}$ with $f$, giving
\[
G(\beta)\sim\sum_{k\ge1}\frac{\kappa_k(r;f)}{k!\,\beta^k},\qquad \kappa_1=\Cov_p(r,f),\quad\kappa_2=\SkewCov_p(r,f),
\]
with $k$-th order remainder $C_k(R/\beta)^{k+1}\sigma_p(f)$ and the corresponding safety statement.

\paragraph{Radius of convergence.} What is the growth of $C_k$, and does the series converge for $R/\beta<\rho$ with explicit $\rho$?

\paragraph{Structure of the hacked set.} Find checkable hypotheses under which $H(\varepsilon)$ is an interval, so the transition is a genuine bifurcation rather than a supremum.

\paragraph{Vector-valued and worst-case audits.} For $\mathcal F$ with $\sigma_p(f)\le1$, the worst-case first-order drift is $\sup_{f\in\mathcal F}|\Cov_p(r,f)|/\beta$; for the whole unit ball this is $\sigma_p(r)/\beta$. A uniform threshold theorem over classes $\mathcal F$ would extend the result from one audit to a battery.

\paragraph{Estimation theory.} What sample complexity certifies $\beta\ge(1+\delta)\hat C/\varepsilon$ with confidence $1-\alpha$, given a plug-in estimate $\hat C$?

\paragraph{Continuous and sequential settings.} Extend to measurable response spaces and to token-level policies where the KL penalty is applied per step, so the effective tilt compounds along the trajectory.

\section{Conclusion}

The audit gap of a KL-regularized policy is a covariance---exactly at every regularization strength against the likelihood ratio, and to leading order against the reward itself, with an explicit second-order remainder and a matching lower bound. From that identity a genuine phase transition follows: at auditor tolerance $\varepsilon$, hacking of a statistic switches on at critical regularization strength
\[
\beta_c(\varepsilon)=(1+o(1))\frac{|\Cov_p(r,f)|}{\varepsilon}.
\]
The second-order coefficient is the skew covariance, and the biased two-point model shows the expansion cannot be improved. Reward hacking is predictable \emph{before} training, from correlations measurable on the base model alone---and the standard knob of alignment practice, the KL penalty, has a formula.

